fpcontrols.patch - the controls of the views that look along the ground (chase, shoulder, first
person): mouse and right-stick look, movement relative to the look, the character turned to the
look, the data a HUD needs.  This file holds the changes to files other work owns;
docs/issues/native-first-person-controls.md says what it is and what was run.

Made against tools/recomp WITH app/fpview.patch applied (the camera base of 13:28; the line
"base" below).  Apply from tools/recomp, after fpview.patch:

    cd tools/recomp && patch -p1 --dry-run < app/fpcontrols.patch && patch -p1 < app/fpcontrols.patch

The new files it needs are already in place and are not in the patch (nothing compiles them
until app/build.py is patched):
    app/fpcontrols.h  app/fpcontrols.cpp   the controls: plain C++, no window system, nothing of
                                           the runtime but its memory (iface.h)
    app/look_host.h  app/look_mac.mm       the pointer hidden and held as the look (macOS)

Notes for the merge
 - Nothing in hle.txt, rt/, render/, net/, sound/: no new translation, no change to the game.
   The simulation is given ordinary controller rows and the game camera's yaw word; with the
   view "overhead" (the default) or "action", fpcontrols::translate changes nothing in a row.
 - app/fpview.h, fpview.cpp (the camera work's files): ONE addition, fpview::add_pitch(degrees) -
   an atomic that is added to the selected view's pitch at the next draw pass, inside the
   view's own limits - so that a mouse can look up and down.  12 lines; if the camera work
   adds it itself, drop these two hunks.
 - app/app_main.cpp: the last step of Pads::read tells fpcontrols the view of the last draw
   pass and lets it translate the row (in a look view the right stick and L3 are withheld
   from the game: its own camera-stick code would turn the same yaw word slowly and step the
   hidden zoom - so --opt view-zoom=off is never needed); a call-out of fpview::on_view
   turns the first-person picture onto the walking direction (2.48 degrees) and adds the
   kick of swings and hits; mouse buttons and wheel of the window (not of a test window);
   Escape gives a held pointer back; a 50 ms timer holds the pointer while a look view is
   played in the window that has the keyboard (never in a test window, never off screen);
   three more key names (Space = Square, Shift = R1, Tab = R3); "app fpc ..." for scripts
   and the control socket; settings lines "controls.*"; the report's last lines.
 - app/settings.h: "controls.*" lines are kept with the "view" lines and handed on.
 - app/help_mac.mm: the keys panel ends with fpcontrols::help_text().
 - app/build.py: fpcontrols.cpp and look_mac.mm are compiled; help_mac.mm is given -I rt.
 - app/win_main.cpp, app/build_win.py (compiled and linked here with build_win.py; NOTHING
   of it was run): fpcontrols.cpp is compiled into the Windows program; the row goes through
   fpcontrols::translate (it passes unchanged until the Windows program has the views and
   calls fpcontrols::set_view - fpview.patch's own note says how the views get there); raw
   mouse input, the pointer clipped and hidden, mouse buttons and wheel, the three key
   names, "app fpc", the report line.
